estion": "Out to Win chronicled the history of which former professional baseball player?", "answer": "William Daro Bean", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who commissioned the documentary about the investiture of the Duke of Cornwall?", "answer": "the Queen", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "When were the comedy team starring in Shivering Sherlocks active?", "answer": "1928 until 1970", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who created the production company that will produce the Hugo Award winning short story, Flowers for Algernon?", "answer": "Alan Polsky and Gabe Polsky", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "How many decades did the career of the English film director and producer who founded Woodfall Film Productions span?", "answer": "five", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What chess champion who holds the records for consecutive professional tournament victories (15) was featured in the World Chess Championship 1984?", "answer": "Garry Kasparov", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Are Rockefeller University and Massachusetts Institute of Technology both considered to be prestigious?", "answer": "yes", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "When was the album that has the song \"Piggy\" released?", "answer": "March 8, 1994", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The Dollar is the major-label debut album from an American country that was born in what year", "answer": "1975", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Eli Ginzberg is the son of Professor of Talmud, at the Jewish Theological Seminary of America known for his participation in what movement?", "answer": "Conservative Movement of Judaism", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Were both  Robert Pinsky and Marina Tsvetaeva translators?", "answer": "no", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "How many specials were aired on the series that Buddy Van Horn doubled for Guy Williams on?", "answer": "4 hour-long specials", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "are Meet the Patels and Arakimentari the same genre of film", "answer": "yes", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "What is the nationality of the thriller fiction author who used the Rose Line as inspiration for his well known 2003 novel?", "answer": "American", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What highway gives access to the town named after Joseph Cutler?", "answer": "State Route 191", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which publication has run longest, Signs of the Times or Space Science Fiction?", "answer": "Space Science Fiction was a science fiction magazine published by Space Publications, Inc. of New York and The Archer Press Ltd. of London that ran for eight issues from May 1952 to September 1953.", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "What was the name of the English singer who sang the song Supercalifragilisticexpialidocious?", "answer": "Julie Andrews", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Johnetta \"Netta\" Elzie is an American civil rights activist, she is one of the leaders in the activist group We The Protesters and co-edits the Ferguson protest newsletter \"This Is the Movement\" with fellow activist DeRay Mckesson, an American civil rights activist and former school administrator, and supporter of which movement?", "answer": "Black Lives Matter", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who directed the television drama for which the actress Cathryn Bradshaw is perhaps best known ?", "answer": "Beeban Kidron.", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "John Carroll Power served as the first custodian of a tomb of a president and how many of his sons ?", "answer": "three of their four sons", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "If Serissa is a genus of a flowering plant, what is Westringia a genus of?", "answer": "Westringia is a genus of Australian shrubs.", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Which band formed earlier, Nerf Herder or The Nymphs?", "answer": "The Nymphs", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Walker Buehler plays for which baseball team established in 1883 in Brooklyn, New York?", "answer": "Los Angeles Dodgers", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which English actress was introduced in an episode of Jonathan Creek?", "answer": "Sheridan Smith", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Galgameth was a loose remake of the 1985 film released in what country?", "answer": "North Korea", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "According to whom was Yuri Izrael the  \"most influential scientific adviser\" to the current President of Russia?", "answer": "CNN", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Ice Princess and In Search of the Castaways, are entertainment of what type?", "answer": "film", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "The Anne Frank tree, was a horse-chestnut tree,in the city center of Amsterdam that was featured in Anne Frank's \"The Diary of a Young Girl\", Anne Frank described the tree from which writer's house and biographical museum dedicated to Jewish wartime diarist Anne Frank?", "answer": "Anne Frank House", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Frank Galton was once secretary to the founder of what educational establishment?", "answer": "London School of Economics", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who was the lyricist who wrote over 51% of the music for the Musical \"Once\"  that won the 2013 Grammy Award for Best Musical Theater Album?", "answer": "Glen Hansard and Mark\u00e9ta Irglov\u00e1", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Bad4Good consisted of a singer who was the voice of which character on Tiny Toon Adventures?", "answer": "Montana Max", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "How long was the trial in which Thomas Fitch defended Morgan Earp?", "answer": "a month-long", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "David Self was the screenwriter for the 1999 film directed by whom?", "answer": "Jan de Bont", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What science fiction character featured in \"Doctor Who\" is featured in an original novel written by Christopher Bulis?", "answer": "First Doctor", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who's 6th album named Press to Play contained the song Stranglehold?", "answer": "Paul McCartney", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What textbook was written by the economist who created the \"welfare definition\" of economics?", "answer": "Principles of Economics", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What year was the film made that was about the inventor of a type of passive solar house that is made of both natural and upcycled materials such as earth-packed tires?", "answer": "2007", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What is the nationality of the player who scored 50 goals for the Pittsburgh Penguins in their 2011-12 season?", "answer": "Russian", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What company published both The Scorch Trials and The Death Cure?", "answer": "Delacorte Press", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "William Gaddis and Virginia Woolf, are Americans?", "answer": "no", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Which airport offers no passenger airline services since United Express ended flights to San Francisco; Cherry Capital Airport or Chico Municipal Airport?", "answer": "Chico Municipal Airport", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "German economist Anita Bose Pfaff and Emilie Schenkl are both immediate family members of what major leader of Indian nationalism?", "answer": "Subhas Chandra Bose", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Of these two animated features, A Christmas Carol or Munto, which is a maga adaptiation?", "answer": "Munto", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "What bodybuilder commonly stayed at the muscles house and was known for playing Hercules, Goliath, Sandokan in  Italian-made peplum films?", "answer": "Stephen Lester \"Steve\" Reeves", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What stars of \"Everybody Loves Raymond\" studied acting at The Neighborhood Playhouse School of the Theatre in New York City?", "answer": "Doris Roberts", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "In what year was the company of which Richard J. Daly is the President and Chief Executive Officer founded?", "answer": "2007", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What year was the American drama, starring Kiff VandenHeuvel and directed by Steven Soderbergh, released?", "answer": "2013", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The Children's National Medical Center and the Psychiatric Institute of Washington both care for what age group?", "answer": "children", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "How are Carnegie Mellon University and University of Virginia alike?", "answer": "research university", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Skunk Anansie and 7 Year Bitch are both the names of which type of groups?", "answer": "rock band", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "\"The Gate\" is a song recorded by Icelandic musician Bj\u00f6rk, it was released on 15 September 2017 through One Little Indian as the lead single from her ninth studio album, Utopia (2017), by Icelandic musician Bj\u00f6rk, scheduled for release in November 2017 through which organization?", "answer": "One Little Indian Records", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Greek fire provided a technilogical advantage and victory during the salvation of what city which is also the capital city of the Roman/Byzantine from two Arab sieges?", "answer": "Constantinople", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Joel H. Cohen is a Canadian writer for \"Saturday Night Live\", \"Suddenly Susan\" and \"The Simpsons\", and is the younger brother of which one-time \"Simpsons\" writer, a Canadian comedy writer and director?", "answer": "Robert Cohen", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "When was the founder of a hedge fund in 1996 notable for short selling Lehman born?", "answer": "1968", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What director who holds both American and Italian citizenship, appeared opposite in the comedy thriller \"Midnight Run\" of the actor who played FBI agent Alonzo Mosely?", "answer": "Robert Anthony De Niro", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What was the name of the famous event where Lithuanian Nazi collaborators killed 100,000 in Paneriai?", "answer": "The Ponary massacre", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who developed the housing estates near New Town Plaza that includes 4 high-rise buildings and a shopping arcade in 1988?", "answer": "Henderson Land Development", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What place with a population of 122,041 has a place in Walden County Florida with a similar name?", "answer": "Miramar, Florida", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The author of Kuhn vs. Popper was born in what year?", "answer": "1959", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Tony Harrison created renditions of the ancient Greek dramatic work Oresteia written by which author?", "answer": "Aeschylus", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who is the \"Father of the Soviet Symphony\" who wrote The Symphony No. 1 in C minor?", "answer": "Nikolai Myaskovsky", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Wanda Nara is the sister of which Argentine model?", "answer": "Zaira Tatiana Nara", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "In which year did this American pioneering mountaineer and writer, after whom this scattering of glaciers and snowfields located on the southern flank of Mount Rainier in Washington was named, climb Mount Rainier?", "answer": "1870", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who founded the network on whhich Top Model po-russki aired?", "answer": "Sergey Lisovskiy", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What is the name of the award-winning feature-length documentary film that follows the real-life adventures of a non-profit battle gaming and live-action role-playing (LARP) club in the United States based in the Baltimore/Washington/Virginia area?", "answer": "Darkon", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Phil Boyle's district includes what village in the Town of Islip on Fire Island?", "answer": "Ocean Beach", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which Disney film was released first, Monkeys, Go Home! or Superdad?", "answer": "Monkeys, Go Home!", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "When did the second season of the series which has the first titled \"It Came from the Nightosphere\" premiered? ", "answer": "October 11, 2010", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The Beast Reawakens opens with a quote from an autobiographical account of the experiences of British soldier T. E. Lawrence because it was the favorite book of what Nazi commando?", "answer": "SS-Standartenf\u00fchrer Otto Skorzeny", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "When was the University founded at which Ibrahim Garba is a professor ? ", "answer": "October 4, 1962,", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Bret Alan Stafford is a former American football player, who records have since been surpassed by which American football quarterback, who is currently a free agent?", "answer": "Vincent Paul Young Jr.", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The Pro-Am Poker Equalizer is a televised poker tournament in which professional poker players play against celebrities which began airing on ESPN in January 2007, what is this variation of the card game of poker called?", "answer": "Texas hold 'em", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What is the name of the manager of the band that released the album \"Dan Neramit\" in April 2013?", "answer": "Thaneth Warakulnukroh", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What Italian professional football club, founded in 1899, was the first to establish an ultras group?", "answer": "A.C. Milan", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "In 2012 and 2015 Pine Valley Golf Club was ranked the number one golf course in the World by a magazine founded in what year?", "answer": "1959", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Red is a series of American action comedy films starring an English actor who began her acting career with with company?", "answer": "Royal Shakespeare Company", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Where did the religion of Mountain Wolf Woman originate?", "answer": "U.S. State of Oklahoma", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Star One (also referred to as Arjen Anthony Lucassen's Star One) is a progressive metal supergroup/side-project of Arjen Anthony Lucassen of Ayreon fame, Ayreon is a musical project by which nationality songwriter, singer, multi-instrumentalist musician and record producer, Arjen Anthony Lucassen?", "answer": "Dutch", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Casualties of War used a source from what Conde Nast magazine as its backing?", "answer": "The New Yorker", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Third Window Films was a distributor for the 2002 South Korean film directed by whom?", "answer": "Lee Chang-dong", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What is the oldest section from where Robert Hay was born?", "answer": "Norman Keep or Pele Tower", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "How tall is the gravestone monument located on the largest island of New Hampshire?", "answer": "46.5-foot-tall", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Question: What characters did the focus of the 2011 documentary I Am Nancy play in the sitcom \"Growing Pains\"?", "answer": "Marie Lubbock and Amy Boutilier", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "How many times did the murder victim of an heir to the Du Pont family fortune win Olympic Medals?", "answer": "seven-time", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Have both Dariush Mehrjui and David Gordon Green worked as directors?", "answer": "yes", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Susan Eisenhower's father served in what capacity for the United States Army?", "answer": "United States Army officer and military historian", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Brotherhood of the Wolf, is a 2001 French historical action horror film directed by Christophe Gans, stars included in it is which American actor and martial artist, and  is perhaps best known for his roles as Mani in the French film \"Brotherhood of the Wolf\"?", "answer": "Mark Dacascos", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The Sting starred which \"Thirteenth Aunt\" from the \"Once Upon a Time in China\" series?", "answer": "Kwan Chi-lam", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Does Empire of the Sun or 3 Doors Down have more band members?", "answer": "3 Doors Down", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "What member of the original cast of \"Rent\" starred in Temptation?", "answer": "Adam Pascal", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which character is married to the Vision but also the daughter of Magneto?", "answer": "The character first appeared in \"X-Men\" #4 (March 1964)", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What is the name of the current party leader that Aasmund Frisak was a politician for?", "answer": "Erna Solberg", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What word concluded the speech given at the Logie Awards attended by the actress who played Peggy Fair on Mannix?", "answer": "a swear word", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Of the two fottballer O'Leary brothers, which is the oldest?", "answer": "David O'Leary", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Laminacauda insulana is a species of sheet weaver found in Tristan da Cunha, colloquially Tristan, is the name of both a remote group of volcanic islands in the south Atlantic Ocean and the main island of that group, the nearest continental land, is which country  ?", "answer": "South Africa", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which Norwegian actress was one of the first entrants on stage at the 1994 Winter Olympics?", "answer": "Liv Ullmann", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Ahn Nae-sang, the South Korean actor had a supporting role in Lee Chang-dong's 2002 movie about a romance between a disabled man released from prison and a woman with cerebral palsy; what is the name?", "answer": "Oasis", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The actor that played Kim Hyde in the series \"Home and Away\" stars with Natalie Portman and Anthony Hopkins in a 2013 superhero film directed by who?", "answer": "Alan Taylor", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which of the video games in which Troy Baker has his voice was developed by Atlus for PlayStation 3 and Xbox 360? ", "answer": "Catherine", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "When was the one of the  social network on which Bananagrams was released, launched? ", "answer": "2005", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which genus contains more officially recognized species, Oplismenus or Abrus?", "answer": "Abrus", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": "Vibulenus Agrippa was accused of treason under the reign of the Emperor who was leader during which years?", "answer": "14 AD to 37 AD", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What did lieutenant generals Millard Harmon and Frank Maxwell Andrews have in common?", "answer": "United States Army Air Forces", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Red Saunders worked for nearly two decades for a magazine included with what newspaper?", "answer": "\"The Sunday Times\"", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The Westerner starred which actor who won three Academy Awards?", "answer": "Walter Andrew Brennan", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What is the name of the third album that was released by the same artist behind L.E.S Artistes?", "answer": "99\u00a2", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who may be the largest manufacturer of maize flour?", "answer": "Gruma", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The son of Peter Dancy directed which 2011 American thriller drama film?", "answer": "Martha Marcy May Marlene", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Where is the polar region which Ringed seals live in located at", "answer": "northernmost part of Earth", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Where was the NBA draft that had the Chicago Bulls picking Lari Arthur as the 49th overall pick held?", "answer": "MCI Center", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "The Cleveland Cavaliers first began play in the NBA in 1970, as an expansion team under the ownership of Nick Mileti, Jerry Tomko, the father of future Major League Baseball pitcher Brett Daniel Tomko, submitted the winning entry to name the team the \"Cavaliers\" through a competition sponsored by \"The Plain Dealer\", Tomko is an American former professional baseball pitcher, played in which league?", "answer": "Major League Baseball", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Eisenwerke Oberdonau was created after what annexation of Austria into Nazi Germany?", "answer": "Anschluss", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "From 1983 to 1993, Billy Brewer was the head football coach at a univeristy located in what city?", "answer": "Oxford", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Jabberwocky was a poem included in the sequel to the fantasy novel written in what year?", "answer": "1865", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Which submachine gun built by Societe Luxembourgeoise SA in Luxembourg between 1954 and 1957 was capable of using magazines developed in Nazi Germany?", "answer": "The Sola", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Did Carlos Saldanha and Phil Joanou both direct films?", "answer": "yes", "type": "comparison"}, {"question": " Kazoku no Katachi starred the actress and designer who is from what country?", "answer": "Japan", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Who co-wrote the song White Light with the English singer, songwriter and record producer who was a member of the musical duo Wham! in the 1980s?", "answer": "George Michael", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Rounding out the top 5 at the 2014 Bank of America 500 were the 2009 NASCAR nationwide series champion, and what two others?", "answer": "Jamie McMurray, Joey Logano", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "St Mary's Catholic High School, Grimsby and Matthew Humberstone School joined to form what school?", "answer": "St. Andrew's College", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "In what year did the director of a 2001 British film starring Mark Rylance and Kerry Fox serve as president of the jury at the Cannes festival?", "answer": "2003", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "Where was the owner of the Clermont Club born?", "answer": "Delhi", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "R-Mean is most recognized for his single \"Lost Angels\" featuring an American rapper who was born where?", "answer": "Compton, California", "type": "bridge"}, {"question": "What type of sport does Heisman curse and Jason White have in common?", "answer": "football", "type": "bridge"}, {"question":